WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.179
Locking makes a reader wait for a writer.

00:00:06.179 --> 00:00:10.359
Multiversion concurrency control takes the other route: keep several versions of each row.

00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:14.538
A reader takes the version it is allowed to see, and never waits.

00:00:14.538 --> 00:00:18.718
Readers and writers stop blocking.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:22.459
Picture a team of ten engineers on one codebase.

00:00:22.459 --> 00:00:26.199
You would never lock the whole repository so only one of them can edit at a time.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:29.940
Git lets them all work at once, each on the version they pulled.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.680
A database can do the same.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.421
Watch one repository over time.

00:00:37.421 --> 00:00:41.161
It starts at version one, the base.

00:00:41.161 --> 00:00:44.902
Engineer B pulls early and gets version one.

00:00:44.902 --> 00:00:48.642
Then Engineer C commits a change, creating version two, the base plus the diff.

00:00:48.642 --> 00:00:52.383
Engineer D pulls afterward and gets version two.

00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:56.123
But B, who pulled before the commit, still holds version one.

00:00:56.123 --> 00:00:59.864
When you pulled decides what you see, and nobody waited on anybody.

00:00:59.864 --> 00:01:03.920
Two rules you already believe.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.975
First, read only committed values, never a write still in flight.

00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:12.031
Second, keep it compact: store only the diff between versions, the way git does.

00:01:12.031 --> 00:01:16.616
A snapshot is not a copy of the database.

00:01:16.616 --> 00:01:21.201
It is one number, a timestamp, taken the moment your transaction begins.

00:01:21.201 --> 00:01:25.786
Nothing is duplicated, nothing grows ten times larger.

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:30.371
From that timestamp on, you read every value as it stood at that instant.

00:01:30.371 --> 00:01:34.956
Take one value V, committed at two o'clock.

00:01:34.956 --> 00:01:39.540
Minnie starts overwriting it at two forty-five, but does not commit the new value until three oh three.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.125
In that window the write is in flight.

00:01:44.125 --> 00:01:48.710
Pluto, with a snapshot of two fifty, reads the old value.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:53.295
Mickey, at three o'clock, also reads the old value, never the half-written one.

00:01:53.295 --> 00:01:57.880
Only a query whose snapshot lands after the commit, at three oh five, reads the new value.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.465
The write becomes visible at the commit, not when it starts.

00:02:02.465 --> 00:02:06.286
Those versions add up.

00:02:06.286 --> 00:02:10.106
Every update commits a new value and keeps the old one behind, each stamped with its commit time.

00:02:10.106 --> 00:02:13.927
Here, four versions deep, committed from two o'clock to three ten.

00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:17.748
MVCC must keep an old version while any running query still reads it.

00:02:17.748 --> 00:02:21.569
So look at the running queries.

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:25.389
The one that opened at two fifty still reads v3, and newer queries read v4.

00:02:25.389 --> 00:02:29.210
Nobody is still reading v1 or v2, the oldest committed values.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.031
They are dead.

00:02:33.031 --> 00:02:36.852
Vacuum is the garbage collector.

00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:40.672
It discards those older values, greyed out here, and frees the space.

00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:44.493
Without it, versions would pile up forever.

00:02:44.493 --> 00:02:49.114
MVCC is the default in every database, but its default level is not quite serializable.

00:02:49.114 --> 00:02:53.734
Two snapshots can commit changes that together break, a gap called write skew, which serializable isolation closes.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:58.355
Readers never block.

00:02:58.355 --> 00:03:02.976
The only lock is writer to writer.

00:03:02.976 --> 00:03:06.929
Here is the payoff.

00:03:06.929 --> 00:03:10.882
Why not run the year-end analytics on the production database?

00:03:10.882 --> 00:03:14.836
It is not lock contention.

00:03:14.836 --> 00:03:18.789
MVCC fixed that.

00:03:18.789 --> 00:03:22.742
A ten-minute analytical scan saturates CPU and IO, pollutes the cache hot queries depend on, and pins vacuum for ten minutes while tables bloat.

00:03:22.742 --> 00:03:26.696
None of those is a lock.

00:03:26.696 --> 00:03:30.649
The fix is physical separation.

00:03:30.649 --> 00:03:34.602
Copy the data to a separate columnar warehouse, and keep the transactional database user-facing only.

00:03:34.602 --> 00:03:38.354
Multiversion concurrency control is the default in every modern database.

00:03:38.354 --> 00:03:42.107
Keep versions, read by snapshot, and readers and writers stop fighting.

00:03:42.107 --> 00:03:45.859
One model is left.

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:49.612
Optimistic concurrency control bets clashes are rare and takes no locks.

00:03:49.612 --> 00:03:53.364
That is next.
